<div class="oi-tips" style="display:grid;gap:8px;font-size:13px;color:var(--d-ink)">
    <strong style="font-size:14px">How to write great replies</strong>
    <div><strong>1. Always give them a next step.</strong> If we don't have it, offer a similar record we do have, the other store, a special order ("I can order it for you, about 1 to 2 weeks"), or "I'll text you as soon as one comes in."<br><em>Great reply:</em> "We don't have Invincible on vinyl right now, but I can special order it for you, or text you the moment a used copy comes in. Which would you like?"</div>
    <div><strong>2. Write full, friendly sentences.</strong> Capital letters and correct spelling: "don't", "I'm sorry". Answer "ok" or a thumbs up with a warm line like "Great, see you soon!" or "Perfect, thank you!"</div>
    <div><strong>3. Use their name and yours.</strong> "Hi Joseph, it's Fatteen from Nivessa!" People love knowing who they're talking to.</div>
    <div><strong>4. Give the exact detail.</strong> Price, store, hours, tracking link, date. "It's $28 at our Hollywood store, open until 1am tonight. I'll hold it under your name until tomorrow."</div>
    <div><strong>5. Turn problems into happy customers.</strong> Say sorry once, then offer a swap or a fix and say exactly what happens next and when.</div>
    <div><strong>6. Answer within an hour.</strong> Need time? Send "Looking this up for you now!" and follow up the same day.</div>
    <div><strong>7. Keep staff chats in Slack.</strong> Shifts, training and work questions go to Slack so the store numbers stay for customers.</div>
    <div><strong>8. Close the loop.</strong> When they say "thanks" or "ok", you're done: click <strong>Fixed it</strong> (or Resolve in the Communications Hub) so it leaves the list.</div>
</div>
